// Actually, at t=1100, the window is [100, 1100]. We have requests at 100,200, and now 1100 -> three, so allowed.
// Let's try to add another at t=1100 -> should be false because we already have three.
console.log(`t=${t}:`, limiter2.tryAcquire(t)); // false (because we already added one at 1100? Wait, we just tried to acquire and it returned true and added? Let's adjust.)

// Better to reset and do step by step with a fresh limiter.

console.log('\nTest 3: Reset and step-by-step');
const limiter3 = createRateLimiter(2, 1000);
let time = 0;
console.log(`t=${time}:`, limiter3.tryAcquire(time)); // true (count=1)
time += 500;
console.log(`t=${time}:`, limiter3.tryAcquire(time)); // true (count=2)
time += 500; // now time=1500
console.log(`t=${time}:`, limiter3.tryAcquire(time)); // false (still two in window [500,1500]? Wait, window is 1000 ms, so at time=1500, window is [500,1500]. We have requests at 0 and 500 and 1500? Actually we haven't added 1500 yet. We have requests at 0 and 500. The request at 0 is out of window because 0 < 500? Yes, 0 < 500 (window start). So at time=1500, the window is [500,1500]. We have only one request at 500 (since 0 is out). So we should be able to acquire.
// Let's recalc: We have two requests: at 0 and 500. At time=1500, window start = 1500-1000=500. So we remove requests <500. The request at 0 is removed, leaving only the request at 500. So count=1, then we add the new one at 1500 -> count=2 -> allowed.
// So the above step is wrong. Let's do a corrected test.

